(3247580, 449859, 71651, '<td valign="top">Looking back I realize I should have probably taken the knife and the sippy cup glass out of the picture. I was trying to make it look like the "dirty secret" was that I was making apple juice in a kids cup from a rotten apple. It would have looked a lot better if it has just been the apple in the shot.</td>', 'Looking back I realize I should have probably taken the knife and the sippy cup glass out of the picture. I was trying to make it look like the "dirty secret" was that I was making apple juice in a kids cup from a rotten apple. It would have looked a lot better if it has just been the apple in the shot.', '2007-01-17 15:25:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247605, 449638, 46314, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by eyewave:</b></div><hr/><i> Congrats on your top10 finish! I can imagine this had scored even higher in b/w-portrait </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Yeah, I wondered about that when I entered it... I just did a quick check: if I''d gotten exactly the same score in the B&amp;W challenge I would have ended in exactly the same position: 9th. So at least I can be content about that :-)</td>', 'Originally posted by eyewave: Congrats on your top10 finish! I can imagine this had scored even higher in b/w-portrait   Yeah, I wondered about that when I entered it... I just did a quick check: if I''d gotten exactly the same score in the B&W challenge I would have ended in exactly the same position: 9th. So at least I can be content about that :-)', '2007-01-17 15:28:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247680, 450827, 56797, '<td valign="top">looks very fiery. fantastic finish too.</td>', 'looks very fiery. fantastic finish too.', '2007-01-17 15:39:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247699, 449929, 39007, '<td valign="top">Well don''t let your wife think the score has anything to do with the model - she has a very beautiful body (she has a very similar build to my wife who features in my first DPC entry, I love curves lol)<br/> <br/>Its just that DPC voters are very touchy about these types of photos and they either score high or score low - they tend to get a strong reaction one way or the other so midrange results are rare...</td>', 'Well don''t let your wife think the score has anything to do with the model - she has a very beautiful body (she has a very similar build to my wife who features in my first DPC entry, I love curves lol) Its just that DPC voters are very touchy about these types of photos and they either score high or score low - they tend to get a strong reaction one way or the other so midrange results are rare...', '2007-01-17 15:42:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247725, 448696, 63345, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the comments.  Yes, this is my first DPC challenge and must give Zoomdak my younger brother a large amount of the credit as he guided me on the editing.  On another note, the horizon line is actually straight, as charliebaker noted the bright logs create an illusion that it is not.</td>', 'Thanks for all the comments.  Yes, this is my first DPC challenge and must give Zoomdak my younger brother a large amount of the credit as he guided me on the editing.  On another note, the horizon line is actually straight, as charliebaker noted the bright logs create an illusion that it is not.', '2007-01-17 15:47:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247757, 450442, 66952, '<td valign="top">Excellent attempt for a first challenge. Very artful and well thought out. I think you''re right, the person and bright spot on the right side needs to be dropped out. If I were taking the same subject, I would actually have gone the opposite direction, dropped the ISO as low as it would go, closed down the aperture, and gone for an even longer exposure (on a tripod, of course). This is assuming that you are have a model that you can get to stand in the corner and stay still for the full length of the exposure.</td>', 'Excellent attempt for a first challenge. Very artful and well thought out. I think you''re right, the person and bright spot on the right side needs to be dropped out. If I were taking the same subject, I would actually have gone the opposite direction, dropped the ISO as low as it would go, closed down the aperture, and gone for an even longer exposure (on a tripod, of course). This is assuming that you are have a model that you can get to stand in the corner and stay still for the full length of the exposure.', '2007-01-17 15:56:09.000000', NULL, 0),
